Forecasting Hourly Energy Demand in Seattle
Presentation and Paper

Problem Statement
Can I predict how much energy Seattle will need on a given hour, based on weather conditions and holidays?

Why It Matters
This would matter to local utilities like Seattle City Light, regional operators, energy traders, and policymakers.
Accurate forecasting allows utilities to rely efficiently on variable energy sources and manage loads effectively during weather shifts.
If utilities overproduce electricity, then they waste fuel, which costs money.
Underproducing may lead to blackouts, which pose safety risks and disruption.

What Would Success Look Like
A success would be an accurate time series prediction that predict Seattle's hourly energy demand a day or two in advance.
It would utilize historical energy data, local weather, and events.

Data Sources
1) Hourly Electric Grid Monitor
www.eia.gov/electricity/gridmonitor/dashboard/electric_overview/regional/REG-NW
Provides: The EIA provides historical energy load data for the Seattle area.
Analyzing a long enough timeframe would get us at least 200K rows of data. It is complete and very recent
Uncertain: Accessing the whole data requires API key which is free to register.
Excel online cannot open the more than 25MB of data in it.
It may have missing data, outages, and metering errors.

2) Historical Weather API
open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-forecast-api
Provides: It provides hourly historic weather data for a location of your choosing. I chose Seattle.
Is complete, recent, and accessible.
Uncertain: I will need to determine if looking at one location is enough, or if I have to look at multiple.
If dataset is enreliable, I will use the NOAA/NWS SeaTac Weather History

3) Holidays and Events Calendar
pypi.org/project/holidays/
Provides: Thankfully there is a python library that provides a list of recognized holidays and their dates for most countries.
It is complete, but somewhat recent and accessible.
Uncertain: Some unrecognized holidays like April Fool's may not be on it. Sport events will not be included.
It will be hard to weight each holiday, as soem are more common than others.

If data source 1 of Seattle area is too unpredicatable, then I will broaden it and search for the whole Pacific Northwest.

Techniques
This would require a time series analysis as I am predicting the future by looking at the past.
I would be using linear regression with energy and with time or weather. Possibly events as well.
I will use Python and Pandas, though I will need a refresher on them.

Unknowns
COVID-19 and the rise of remote work may skew the data.
How does extreme weather or blackouts impact demand on energy?
Does weather impact data more, or does events impact it more?
